# Deny by default, then allow back the few files that are actually ours.
#
# This is the only safe shape for this directory. It holds sixteen runner
# installations, and each one carries a `.credentials` and a
# `.credentials_rsaparams` — the RSA private key that authenticates that runner
# to GitHub. It also holds 8.4 GB of runner binaries, a 121 MB release tarball,
# and `_work` trees containing full checkouts of every repo.
#
# An allowlist that starts from `*` cannot leak any of that by accident. A
# denylist enumerating what to exclude would leak the first time a new file
# appeared in a runner directory — and the thing it leaked would be a key.
#
# Because `*` also ignores the runner directories themselves, git never descends
# into them, so the `svc.sh` / `config.sh` / `run.sh` that ship inside each
# runner are not picked up by the `!*.sh` rule below. Only the scripts at the
# top level are.
*

!.gitignore
!.gitattributes
!.node-version
!README.md
!*.sh

# GitHub Actions CI and community files. All paths inside .github/ are listed
# explicitly — a catch-all !.github/**/* would also un-ignore any secrets or
# workflow outputs that landed there accidentally.
!.github/
!.github/workflows/
!.github/workflows/*.yml
!.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/
!.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*.yml
!.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*.md
!.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md
!.github/CODEOWNERS
!.github/dependabot.yml

# Project metadata. Each is named explicitly because `*` denies everything and
# there is no `!*.md` rule at this level — a blanket one would start tracking
# whatever notes happened to be left in this directory.
!LICENSE
!CONTRIBUTING.md
!SECURITY.md
!CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
!SUPPORT.md
!THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md
!CHANGELOG.md
!.node-version

# Host-local settings: GitHub owner, repo list, launchd label prefix, dashboard
# grouping. The example is tracked; the real file is not, because everything in
# it is specific to one machine or one account.
!fleet.env.example

# Repo tooling. `!scripts/` first: with `*` in force git never descends into an
# ignored directory, so a rule for the files inside can never match.
!scripts/
!scripts/*.sh
!scripts/*.py

# Runner job hooks, referenced from each runner's .env. Same directory-first
# rule as above. The admission state these write — .admission/, holding one slot
# file per running job — is runtime and falls through to the `*` by not being
# named here, which is the correct default for a directory whose contents are
# live PIDs.
!hooks/
!hooks/*.sh

# Shell tests for the hooks. The JS side lives in dashboard/test/ with the rest
# of the suite; these are here because what they exercise is bash, run against a
# throwaway fleet root rather than a real runner.
!hooks/tests/
!hooks/tests/*.sh

# runs.sh's discovered repo list. Derived, host-specific, and rebuilt on demand
# — it falls through to the `*` above by not being named here.

# The dashboard. Un-ignore the directory first — with `*` in force, git never
# descends into a directory it has ignored, so a rule for the files inside can
# never match. Then allow back only source extensions: fleet.db, logs/ and
# anything else the daemon writes at runtime stay ignored by falling through.
!dashboard/
!dashboard/lib/
!dashboard/public/
!dashboard/*.js
!dashboard/*.json
!dashboard/*.md
!dashboard/lib/*.js
!dashboard/public/*.js
!dashboard/public/*.css
!dashboard/public/*.html
!dashboard/public/*.webmanifest
!dashboard/public/assets/
!dashboard/public/assets/*.svg

# The test suite. Un-ignored for the same reason the source is: a released
# project whose tests are missing cannot be verified by whoever clones it, and
# every one of these runs against fixtures with no network and no fleet.
!dashboard/test/
!dashboard/test/*.js
!dashboard/test/fixtures/
!dashboard/test/fixtures/*.js

# Re-ignore the runtime alert config. `!dashboard/*.json` above un-ignores it,
# which contradicted the README's claim that it was safe to put a webhook URL
# there — and a webhook URL usually carries a token in the path. The example
# file stays tracked; the real one does not.
dashboard/alerts.config.json

# The auto-remediation bridge. Source in, runtime state out: state.json holds
# per-alert attempt counts, runs/ holds agent transcripts, and repos/ holds
# scratch clones — none of which belong in this repo. They fall through to the
# `*` above by not being un-ignored here, which is why there is no *.json rule.
!dashboard/autofix/
!dashboard/autofix/*.js
!dashboard/autofix/*.sh
!dashboard/autofix/*.md

# Escalation, the one part of this tree with a dependency. Its source and its
# lock file are tracked — the lock especially, because an unattended daemon
# resolving a floating version at install time is how it breaks on a Tuesday.
# node_modules is not un-ignored here, so it keeps falling through to the `*`
# above, and so does the Cursor API key: with a deny-by-default list, a
# credential is excluded because nothing ever allowed it, not because someone
# remembered to name it.
!dashboard/autofix/escalate/
!dashboard/autofix/escalate/*.mjs
!dashboard/autofix/escalate/package.json
!dashboard/autofix/escalate/package-lock.json

# The watchdog. Un-ignoring the directory is what makes the difference here:
# without it `*` kept git from ever descending, so the `!*.sh` rule at the top
# could not match watchctl.sh and neither file was tracked — the source was
# present on disk, absent from the repo, and nothing reported that.
!dashboard/watch/
!dashboard/watch/*.mjs
!dashboard/watch/*.sh

# Documentation handbook. Un-ignore directory first (same reason as above),
# then allow Markdown files. Images go in docs/images/ as optimised PNGs.
!docs/
!docs/*.md
!docs/images/README.md
!docs/images/
!docs/images/*.png
!docs/images/*.svg
!docs/images/*.md

# Generic setup examples. Exact filename set so a real config accidentally
# dropped here (e.g. fleet.env) still falls through to `*` and stays ignored.
!examples/
!examples/*.yml
!examples/*.yaml
!examples/*.json
!examples/*.plist
!examples/fleet.env.single-host
!examples/fleet.env.federated-coordinator
!examples/fleet.env.federated-agent
